From 3c242b350edd1e91dd333b0d4da8a6c14de0b012 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch Author <patch@local>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 04:14:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ai): multi-provider vision gateway for AI meal-photo
 scanning
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds a real, no-fake-fallback AI meal scan feature: photo in, identified
food items out, matched against our own real food database — never
AI-invented nutrition numbers.

Architecture:
- providers/types.ts — shared VisionProvider interface + the honest
  'isFood: false is a valid answer' prompt contract every provider uses
- providers/gemini.provider.ts, groq.provider.ts, openrouter.provider.ts
  — one adapter per provider, each independently Zod-validating its own
  output before it's trusted. Deliberately NO hardcoded default model
  name anywhere — verified during this build that Gemini 1.5 Flash is
  already deprecated and Groq's vision lineup 'changes frequently' per
  their own docs, so model names are env-var-only and a provider simply
  doesn't activate if unconfigured, rather than shipping with a stale
  default.
- gateway.ts — tries providers in order with a per-call timeout and a
  circuit breaker (3 consecutive failures opens a 2-minute cooldown for
  that provider, so a struggling provider doesn't eat a timeout on every
  single request). Adding a 4th/5th free provider later is one new
  adapter file + two env vars, nothing else changes.
- ai.service.ts — enforces a server-side image size ceiling (never
  trusts client-side compression alone), maps the gateway's outcome to
  an honest status (success / not_food / low_confidence /
  all_providers_failed), and best-effort matches each identified item
  against the REAL food database via the existing FoodService search —
  this is what lets nutrition numbers come from a trusted source instead
  of the vision model ever being asked to estimate calories itself.
- meal_scan_events table — observability only. No photo is ever stored,
  anywhere, per product decision — just the outcome (provider used,
  status, confidence, identified item names/quantities).

What this deliberately does NOT do (by design, not oversight):
- No fabricated/mock result when every provider fails — an honest
  all_providers_failed status with a manual-logging message instead
- No rate limiting yet — explicitly deferred until real usage justifies
  it, but the gateway/service boundary is exactly where it plugs in
  later without touching provider code
- No photo storage of any kind, temporary or otherwise

Verified: full workspace build + 96/96 tests passing (50 nutrition-engine
+ 46 API, including 7 new gateway unit tests covering fallback/circuit-
breaker/timeout behavior with fake providers, and 9 new integration tests
against the real route). Also verified live end-to-end against a real
Postgres database with zero AI keys configured: server honestly warns on
startup, the real endpoint returns a clean 200 with an honest
all_providers_failed response (not a 500), and the event was confirmed
sitting in Postgres via an independent psql query.
